   From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)

[seriously edited:]

   Suppose someone is offering you a betting game in which for each dollar
   you put in, you get back either $0.50 or $2.00, with 50-50 odds.  As
   Steve says, this is a positive expected gain - if you do this many itmes,
   you would end up with $1.25 on the average.

   But that's not the best way to go.  If you continually put all your money
   into the bet, you win half and lose half, and end up doing a random walk
   up and down, not getting much of anywhere.

Ahh, but the random walk is your *best* choice.  (Warning: "Best"
means "giving the highest expectation value".)  Although the random
walk leaves you spending half your time in the purgatory of less than
what you invested, during the other half of the time, you are in the
heaven of much, much more than what you invested.  The possibilities
on the up-side are so much higher than 1 that they swamp the
down-side.

Of course, you have to be willing to put up with a *lot* of risk.

Dale

