Subject: LAW: custom vs first principles

> From: drw@bourbaki.mit.edu
> 
>    If I eat and leave a restaurant without paying, our custom says I have
>    committed theft.  Another culture's custom might say otherwise.  Is that a
>    problem?
> 
> It is if that means that the libertarian standard would simply be
> replacing legislated law with customary law.  (If you take your second
> example to the extreme, in a culture where there was no concept of
> "property", there would be no theft.  The libertarian standard would
> then not even guarantee property rights.)

I know of absolutely no human society whatsoever without any concept
of property. Some lack a notion of land ownership, but none, even the
most extreme of communist societies, have no concept of property at all.
(In the Soviet Union, your toothbrush was still your own.)

Although it is true that some nomadic peoples lacked a notion of "who's land"
they usually had pretty strong notions of who's bow and arrow, who's horse, 
etc. Some "primitive" peoples apparently had sufficiently developed property
notions that individual trees were said to have owners.

Property rights tend to get nailed down when it is valuable to do so. Parceling
out broadcast rights would be useless to people without radios. Parceling
out land is useless to people who spend no time in a particular place and
do not exploit natural resources very much. Parceling out air is useless
in a society without pollutants or scarcity of air -- its a very different
matter on a space station.

I won't beat this to death any longer....

Perry

