Date: Tue, 23 Feb 93 12:50:01 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: ECON: trade policy

>Since
>nations have the power to regulate international trade by their
>nationals, we can model international trade as a market with about 180
>participants (or whatever the number of nations is at the moment) --
>each nation's nationals are coerced into acting as agents of the
>"nation's will".

>Dale Worley

I suspect that any economic model that treats States like individual
persons will fail to explain or predict trade policies.  Why?  Because
public choice considerations drive a wedge between State action and
national interests.  The agents who shape statist trade policies seldom act
to maximize the utility of the parties over whom they claim jurisdiction. 
Rather, they seek rents by maximizing the utility of particular interest
groups.

I suppose that if you are in a mood for similies, the proper analogy would
be between a State and an individual who compulsively engages in
self-destructive trade.  Such a State-like individual would, for example,
sell her lungs to feed her belly.  Individual *do* act like this sometimes,
but models of markets usually presume that their participants do not
consistently opt for suicide.


