	Why does it look like a state to you?  It didn't to me.  On sparse
evidence, it looked like something out of _The Machinery of Freedom_.
It was never refered to as a state and considering that Vernor Vinge
has written a story about anarcho-capitalism before, it seems reasonable
that Sjandra Kei could be such a society.  What evidence do you have
that Commercial Security was contracted out by a state?

	A point in your favor that I recall is that there appeared to be
only one Commercial Security.  But, that could be for reasons of coordination
in providing security or it could have been a natural monopoly.  We may
have to ask Mr. Vinge personally to find out the truth (assuming he
decided what that truth was).

	My own view is that Vinge's galaxy was a place of great variety.
You could find everything from a-c to totalitarian theocracies.  If it
can be done, it has been -- somewhere -- somewhen.  Interplanetary
coordination appears to have been aided by arbitration companies,
of which at least some were also military organizations, and so the
superstructure of the galaxy may be viewed as "PPL".  However, this would
only be from the point of view of governments and anarchists.  Subjects
of states would not perceive themselves as living in a PPL society.

	Oh, BTW, I *liked* the ending of the book even though I saw it
a mile off (Mr. Vinge dropped enough hints).  What I *didn't* like was
his statement that civilizations in the Slow Zone inevitably destroyed
themselves after a few thousand years.  THAT bothered me.

	Overall, I loved the book.  I would classify it as extropian
horror because of the presense of evil post-humans/aliens.


