Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

Hal, talking about the double or halves betting/investing scenario:

> What Cover's algorithm for maximizing expected log return does is to
> turn a 25%, wildly varying return into a 6%, rather stable return.
> He has taken a loss in linear return in order to maximize log return.
> That does not sound so smart.

Sounds smart to me.  In the stake everything case you always randomly
walk back to your starting investment value.  There is no benefit to
this strategy.  In the rebalance-portfolio case you don't return.  The
return-to-start probability approaches zero with time.  Your return is
smaller, though assured:

                        linear return    log return
stake everything              25%             0%
rebalance portfolio           12.5%           6%

The log return is a better measure of whether an investment strategy is
sensible, in this case, anyway.  The zero log return is a clear warning
not to expect much from the stake everything strategy, which the linear
return measure does not give.  Caveat Investor!

> Cover alludes to some debate about whether log return is the best
> measure of investment strategy: "A discussion of the naturalness of
> this objective can be found in the series of papers by Williams,
> Kelly, Latane, Breiman, Thorp, Samuelson, Hakansson, Bell and Cover,
> and Arrow."
